export const skill =
  '---\nname: loop\ndescription: Re-run a prompt or skill on a local session schedule until the outcome is met or the user stops it. Use when the user invokes /loop, asks to check or keep working on an interval, or wants a self-paced loop that wakes on an event.\n---\n\n# Loop\n\n`/loop` repeats a prompt in this same agent until `loop_stop`, until the outcome is met, or until the agent is archived. The schedule lives in the loop plugin on this daemon. It ends when the agent is archived, the plugin is unloaded, or the daemon restarts. It is not a cloud automation, not an external cron, and it does not keep running after the agent is gone.\n\nDo not start a background shell that prints `AGENT_LOOP_TICK_` or `AGENT_LOOP_WAKE_`. Do not use OS cron, launchd, or tmux to wake this agent. Do not invent `--max-turns`, `--max-runtime`, or similar flags.\n\n## Syntax\n\n`/loop [interval] <prompt>`\n\n| Form              | Example                              | What you do                                                              |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Leading interval  | `/loop 5m check deploy`              | `loop_arm` mode `fixed`, `everySeconds` 300, then run the prompt now     |\n| Trailing interval | `/loop check deploy every 5 minutes` | same fixed schedule                                                      |\n| No interval       | `/loop work until tests pass`        | `loop_arm` mode `dynamic` after you choose the heartbeat and any watcher |\n| Another skill     | `/loop 5m /foo`                      | fixed schedule whose prompt is `/foo`; each wake, invoke that skill      |\n| Stop              | `/loop stop`                         | `loop_stop`                                                              |\n| Empty             | `/loop`                              | explain this syntax; do not arm                                          |\n\nInterval tokens are `30s`, `5m`, `2h`, and `1d`. Unit words (`seconds`, `minutes`, `hours`, `days`, and short forms such as `sec`, `min`, and `hr`) are the same units. The minimum is 1 second and the maximum is 7 days. A leading interval wins over a trailing one. An interval with no prompt is a usage hint, not a loop.\n\n`loop_arm` does not execute the prompt. You run it once in the current turn. The first fixed tick arrives only after one full interval. A dynamic heartbeat is armed when the turn ends, or immediately when you arm while the agent is already idle.\n\n## Arm\n\nCall `loop_arm` once. A second call replaces the loop. There is only one loop per agent.\n\nFixed:\n\n- `mode`: `fixed`\n- `everySeconds`: the interval in seconds\n- `prompt`: the text to run on each wake, including a skill invocation such as `/foo`\n- optional `purpose`: a lowercase slug; omit it to derive one from the prompt\n\nDynamic, when the user did not give an interval:\n\n- Choose `heartbeatSeconds`. This is the fallback cadence. Prefer a long heartbeat when a watcher will fire on the real event, and a shorter one when the heartbeat is the only wake.\n- Optional `watch`: an argv array, not a shell string. The process is executed directly in this agent\'s working directory with the daemon user\'s permissions. It must print one stdout line only when the event happens (a git ref, a log line, a file change, CI) and otherwise stay quiet. Stderr does not wake the loop. A command that prints continuously will wake this agent on every turn.\n- Then run the prompt now.\n\n`loop_status` reports the armed loop. `get_loop_guide` returns this skill.\n\n## Wake\n\nA later user message is a wake when it contains a sentinel line:\n\n```text\nAGENT_LOOP_TICK_<purpose> {"prompt":"...","reason":"interval"}\n```\n\nor, for a dynamic loop:\n\n```text\nAGENT_LOOP_WAKE_<purpose> {"prompt":"...","reason":"heartbeat"}\n```\n\n`reason` is `watch` when the watcher printed a line. Read the JSON `prompt` and execute that prompt only. Do not call `loop_arm` again for a wake. Do not answer the sentinel as if the user had typed a new request. If the outcome is already met, call `loop_stop` and tell the user; do not keep working. If the user asks to stop, call `loop_stop`.\n\nFixed ticks keep their cadence while you are busy; missed ticks collapse into one wake after the turn. Dynamic mode re-arms the one-shot heartbeat at the end of each turn, and starts the watcher again only if it has exited.\n\n## Stop\n\nCall `loop_stop` when:\n\n- the user asks to stop\n- the outcome in the prompt is met\n- you are replacing the schedule by calling `loop_arm` again\n\nStopping kills the watcher and does not arm the next heartbeat or tick. The user can also run `/loop stop` or press Stop loop in the Loop panel.\n';
